      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey im studying for my security+ exam and im a bit stuck on something. if we are already hashing passwords to keep them secure, why do we even need to use \"salting\"? wouldnt the hash alone be enough to protect it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Salting your passwords adds an extra layer of security by making it significantly harder for attackers to use precomputed hash tables like Rainbow Tables. Even if two users have the same password, their hashed values would be different, reducing the chance of successful attacks. Salting ensures that even if hashed passwords are leaked, it's still computationally intensive to crack them.",
